# Every text file is LF in the repository AND in the working tree.
#
# Without this, a Windows clone with the default `core.autocrlf=true`
# checks files out as CRLF while the index stays LF. Any file a tool then
# writes back as LF is out of step with what autocrlf wants, and git starts
# printing:
#
#   warning: in the working copy of 'x.ts', LF will be replaced by CRLF
#   the next time Git touches it
#
# That warning goes to stderr on ordinary plumbing commands, including
# `git stash create` — and lint-staged reads that command's output as a
# commit SHA. It hands the warnings plus the SHA to `git rev-parse`, which
# fails with "fatal: Needed a single revision", and every commit then aborts
# with "lint-staged failed due to a git error". The commit looks broken by
# whatever you were changing; it is not.
#
# `eol=lf` overrides autocrlf per-repository, so no conversion happens in
# either direction and the warning cannot occur. Every text file in the
# index was already LF when this was added, so it renormalises nothing —
# it only stops the working tree drifting to CRLF on checkout.
#
# There are no .bat/.cmd/.ps1 files here; if one is added it needs an
# explicit `eol=crlf` line, because Windows shells require CRLF.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Kept explicit even though the rule above already covers them: these two
# fail loudly and confusingly (the shell rejects a CRLF script with
# "Exec format error"), so the requirement is worth stating where someone
# editing this file will see it.
.husky/* text eol=lf
*.sh text eol=lf
